  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAnother embedded wave rotating through the upper low over Nova\nScotia will continue chances for showers, mainly over NH through\nthis afternoon. Cyclonic flow aloft and diurnal instability \nwill bring a mix of sun and clouds today and potential for an \nisolated shower. As high pressure attempts to build in from the \nwest with low pressure near Nova Scotia northerly winds will \nremain brisk, particularly over central Maine and the mountains.\nPeak gusts at lower elevations will approach 25 mph with lower \ngusts to the southwest over southern NH. Mostly clear skies \ntonight will favor radiational cooling while winds may limit \ndecoupling. Nevertheless, it will be cool tonight with lows in \nthe 40s and potential for northern valleys to dip into the upper\n30s.\n\nHigh pressure and rising heights will bring fair weather Monday\nand warmer temperatures. Highs will range from the mid 70s north\nto low 80s across the south. A weak cold front will wash out\nover the area Monday night that may have enough moisture for\nsome isolated showers. Otherwise, dry weather continues through\nTuesday with broad high pressure over the Northeast.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nOn Wednesday, an upper-level low over Manitoba will shift to the \nsoutheast and deepen over the northeast US. This will likely allow \nfor a cold front to move across the area Wednesday night, bringing \novernight thunderstorms. Storms could produce wetting rains, but \nshould not be much more than that. Behind the front, a strong \npressure gradient could allow for some breezy northwest winds to \nimpact the region during the day Thursday. High pressure looks to \nmove in for next weekend, with radiational cooling potentially \nallowing for some below average low temperatures then.",
